      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The East Los Angeles Library opened on May 1, 1923 as a modest book collection in a storefront before moving to its current location in 1972.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/East_Los_Angeles%2C_California",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Adjacent to the library is a Japanese library and dojo that helped popularize judo, turning the area into a cultural hub for martial arts.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.laconservancy.org/eastside-l-a/",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Brooklyn Avenue, now known as Cesar E. Chavez Avenue, has long been the heart of East Los Angeles, hosting early labor union offices and community shops.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.laconservancy.org/eastside-l-a/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Belvedere Community Regional Park, built in 1942 as Soledad Park, now offers an Olympic‑size pool, skate park, and splash pad for residents.",
